On Solaris, bdwgc asks mmap for its heap at 0x40000000. In a 64-bit process the
system malloc's heap grows with brk from just above the program, and brk cannot
grow into a mapping: the GC heap at 1 GiB leaves malloc about 940 MiB however
much memory is free (Nix's `nix copy` runs out compressing several paths at
once). Keep the hint for 32-bit processes only; without one, mmap places the GC
heap high in the address space.

--- a/include/private/gcconfig.h
+++ b/include/private/gcconfig.h
@@ -1128,7 +1128,13 @@
         /* doesn't interact correctly with the system malloc.       */
 #   endif
 #   ifdef USE_MMAP
-#     define HEAP_START (ptr_t)0x40000000
+#     ifndef _LP64
+#       define HEAP_START (ptr_t)0x40000000
+#     endif
+        /* A 64-bit process has malloc's heap grow with brk from    */
+        /* just above the program; a GC heap mapped at 1 GiB would  */
+        /* stop it there.  Without a hint the kernel places the GC  */
+        /* heap high up, clear of it.                               */
 #   else
 #     define HEAP_START DATAEND
 #   endif
